// tests/test_device_target_smoke.pdx — mkfs.pdxfs.M4-003 (#15)
// TestDeviceTargetSmoke: STUB smoke, with a documented forward-scope
// deferral. Calls src/format.pdx's `mkfs_format_run_device` directly
// and asserts its documented stub return code.
//
// ================================================================
// Why this cannot be a real device-write smoke test against QEMU
// virtio disk, and why that is a repo-wide, not test-specific, gap
// ================================================================
//
// Issue #15's own title asks for "device-target smoke against QEMU
// virtio disk". `src/format.pdx`'s own module header (mkfs.pdxfs.M3-001,
// #8) already documents, in full, why NO real device-target write path
// exists anywhere in this repo to smoke-test: a repo-tree grep of
// `design/user/syscall-table.md` (paideia-os, refreshed through sysno
// 95) finds exactly ONE block-device-cap-shaped syscall,
// `blkdev_cap_request(bus, devfn, flags)` (sysno 74) -- a cap-MINTING
// call, not a block-granularity open/write/flush op. There is no
// `sys_blkdev_write`/`sys_blkdev_read`-shaped syscall at any number.
// Separately, `src/elevate_wire.pdx`'s `mkfs_elev_require_device_write`
// is a documented fail-closed stub (always `MKFS_ELEV_DENY`) because
// this repo holds no `KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL` broker-endpoint capability
// to construct a real `libpdx-elevate` request with at all (that file's
// own module header). Both gaps are REPO-WIDE substrate absences this
// M4-003 landing cannot manufacture a fixture around -- there is no
// virtio-block-cap this driver could acquire, narrow, or write through
// even inside a real QEMU boot, since the kernel itself has no
// ring-3-reachable block-write syscall yet.
//
// Per this issue's own instruction ("STUB with a documented deferral"),
// this driver therefore does the one thing that IS real and callable at
// this landing: it calls `mkfs_format_run_device` -- the function
// `src/main.pdx`'s device-target dispatch reaches in its (today
// unreachable, since `mkfs_elev_require_device_write` always denies)
// GRANT arm -- directly, bypassing the elevate gate entirely (this
// driver is exercising `mkfs_format_run_device`'s own body, not the
// end-to-end argv-to-elevate-to-format dispatch chain, which always
// resolves to `ELEVATION_DENIED` today regardless of target content --
// see `src/elevate_wire.pdx`'s own module header). This is the
// documented STUB body itself: it parses the device-cap slot (a real,
// working call into `Target::mkfs_dev_parse_slot`) and then
// unconditionally emits `PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1 { result_code:
// DEVICE_TARGET_STUB }` (`FormatRecord::FR_RESULT_DEVICE_TARGET_STUB`,
// 5 -- observable only on stdout via the semantic-pipe fallback line,
// not through this function's own return value) and returns
// `MKFS_EXIT_DEVICE_STUB` (4, a DIFFERENT constant at a different
// layer -- the function's own u64 exit-status contract, not the
// record's `result_code` field). This driver asserts the one layer it
// CAN programmatically observe: `mkfs_format_run_device`'s return
// value.
//
// ================================================================
// Forward-scope deferral (repeated from src/format.pdx / STATUS.md for
// this test file's own visibility)
// ================================================================
//
// A real device-target smoke test against QEMU virtio disk needs, in
// order, per `design/architecture.md` §11.6 (this repo):
//   1. A `sys_cap_query`-equivalent syscall (planned at R13, never
//      implemented) to validate a `cap:blkdev:0xNN` slot actually holds
//      a `KIND_BLKDEV` capability before this repo trusts it.
//   2. A real block-granularity write syscall (`sys_blkdev_write` or
//      equivalent) -- does not exist at any sysno today.
//   3. `mkfs.pdxfs` acquiring a real `KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL`
//      broker-endpoint capability and a coordinated `KIND_BLKDEV`-write
//      `target_cap_kind`/`target_cap_rights` value with osarch, so
//      `src/elevate_wire.pdx`'s stub can be replaced with a real
//      `elevate_client_acquire`/`elevate_client_require` sequence.
// None of these three exist as of this M4 landing. This test file is
// therefore the honest, buildable substitute for the QEMU-virtio smoke
// issue #15 names: it proves the STUB's documented contract holds
// (parses the slot for real, emits the stub record, returns the stub
// exit code) so a future landing of all three substrate items above has
// a driver already in place to extend into a real write-then-readback
// smoke test, rather than writing one from scratch.
//
// ================================================================
// Return-code convention (updated 1.2.0 for issue #26 LE-001)
// ================================================================
//
// Matches this org's established M4-driver convention (see this repo's
// own tests/README.md):
//
//   0 — `mkfs_format_run_device` returned `MKFS_EXIT_ELEVATION_DENIED`
//       (6) verbatim -- the 1.2.0 #26 LE-001 contract holds: the inner
//       scoped-elevate gate (src/elevate_gate.pdx) fail-closes as
//       EX_ELEVATE_REFUSED (both cross-repo blockers still open at this
//       landing -- see that file's module header), so
//       mkfs_format_run_device now takes the ELEVATION_DENIED arm
//       BEFORE the M3-001 DEVICE_TARGET_STUB emit. The historical
//       MKFS_EXIT_DEVICE_STUB (4) return is now on the nominal
//       (unreachable-at-this-landing) row_id arm only -- see
//       src/format.pdx's mkfs_format_run_device header for the pipeline
//       shape.
//   1 — it returned anything else. Two signals a future landing might
//       trigger this:
//         * both cross-repo blockers cleared and a real block-write
//           syscall landed -- expect a 0-or-positive success return
//         * partial resolution (e.g. elevate wired but block-write
//           still absent) -- expect MKFS_EXIT_DEVICE_STUB (4) on the
//           nominal path this driver would then need updated to check
//           for. Either transition means this stub is due for a real
//           write-then-readback smoke replacement.
//
// ================================================================
// paideia-as encoder notes
// ================================================================
//   - No `@no_frame` anywhere.
//   - No `add reg, [mem]` anywhere.
//   - No `test` mnemonic; the only comparison in this file is
//     `cmp rax, 6`, well within the 0x7FFFFFFF cmp-imm bound -- no
//     r10/r11 staging needed.
//   - Cross-file call (`call mkfs_format_run_device;`) uses the bare
//     unqualified symbol name, per this repo's established convention
//     -- an unresolved-external reference in this file's own `.o`,
//     resolved only once a future consumer links src/format.pdx
//     alongside it.
//   - No register survives across the one nested call in this file;
//     no push/pop anywhere.
//   - Reserved-label discipline (project non-negotiable): this file
//     declares no branch label at all (a single straight-line body),
//     so there is no `mkfs_test_`-prefixed label to enumerate.
//
// Upstream: design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §9.1 (paideia-os,
// mkfs.pdxfs.M4-003); src/format.pdx (this repo, mkfs_format_run_device,
// M3-001 #8); src/elevate_wire.pdx (this repo, the fail-closed elevate
// stub M3-001's own dispatch depends on); design/architecture.md §11.1,
// §11.6 (this repo, the full substrate-gap write-up); tests/README.md
// (this repo, return-code convention).

module TestDeviceTargetSmoke = structure {

  // A syntactically valid device-cap URI (11-byte "cap:blkdev:" prefix
  // + a hex slot number) -- never actually resolved against any real
  // capability table by anything this driver calls (see module header:
  // no sys_cap_query-equivalent exists to do so).
  pub let mkfs_test_device_target : [u8; 16] = "cap:blkdev:0x05\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // run() -> u64
  //
  // Calls mkfs_format_run_device directly and asserts its documented
  // stub return code. See module header for the full contract.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let run : () -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{fs} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {fs},
      justification: "mkfs.pdxfs.M4-003 (#15) + 1.2.0 #26 LE-001 update: TestDeviceTargetSmoke::run entry point. rdi=&mkfs_test_device_target (target_ptr), rsi=0 (force_flag, unused by mkfs_format_run_device's body), rdx=1024 (journal_size_blocks, likewise unused), rcx=0 (LV11-025 flags), r8=0 (LV11-025 passphrase_fd), `call mkfs_format_run_device` (src/format.pdx, M3-001 #8 + 1.2.0 #26 LE-001) -- a real, wired call: internally now routes through ElevateGate::mkfs_elevate_open FIRST (src/elevate_gate.pdx, 1.2.0). At this landing that gate fail-closes as EX_ELEVATE_REFUSED (0xFFFFFFFFEB195C4F, per elevate_gate.pdx's module header), so mkfs_format_run_device emits PdxFsFormatRecord@0.1 { result_code: ELEVATION_DENIED } via src/pipe_wire.pdx's mkfs_sp_emit_result, calls mkfs_elevate_close(EX_ELEVATE_REFUSED) -- short-circuits, no fingerprint -- and returns MKFS_EXIT_ELEVATION_DENIED (6). `cmp rax,6; jne mkfs_test_unexpected` (was cmp rax,4 pre-1.2.0). Matching falls to `xor rax,rax; ret` (0: the 1.2.0 #26 LE-001 fail-closed contract holds). mkfs_test_unexpected: `mov rax,1; ret` (1: the contract changed -- see updated return-code convention header for the two shapes that would trip this). No register survives the one nested call, so no push/pop. No `add reg,[mem]`. No `test` mnemonic. No branch label needed beyond mkfs_test_unexpected.",
      block: {
        // LV11-025 (#25) widened mkfs_format_run_device's signature to
        // 5 args (rcx=flags, r8=passphrase_fd). This stub driver's
        // pre-LV11 shape read only rdi/rsi/rdx, but rcx/r8 now carry
        // real caller responsibility even though the stub body still
        // ignores them at this landing -- zero both so a future real
        // device-write body inherits a deterministic starting state.
        lea rdi, [rip + mkfs_test_device_target];
        xor rsi, rsi;                        // force_flag (unused by the body)
        mov rdx, 1024;                       // journal_size_blocks (unused by the body)
        xor rcx, rcx;                        // flags = 0 (LV11-025)
        xor r8,  r8;                         // passphrase_fd = 0 (LV11-025)
        call mkfs_format_run_device;

        cmp rax, 6;                          // MKFS_EXIT_ELEVATION_DENIED (1.2.0 #26 LE-001)
        jne mkfs_test_unexpected;

        xor rax, rax;                        // 0: 1.2.0 #26 LE-001 contract holds
        ret;

      mkfs_test_unexpected:
        mov rax, 1;                          // 1: contract changed -- see header for possible shapes
        ret;
      }
    }
}
